WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.070
PRESENTER: I'm happy to
introduce Demis Hassabis.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:11.010
You probably know, or most
of you know all about him.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:19.200
His bio is he was a
chess prodigy doing

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.190
a PhD in neuroscience.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.970
Then came to MIT.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.200
He founded DeepMind.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.710
They developed the AlphaGo.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.910
They developed-- before
that, Atari Games,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.980
they developed Alpha Zero, and
then more recently AlphaFold,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.180
and yes, in the meantime
received so many prizes,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.630
and so on.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.280
But anyway, that's-- you
can read all of this.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.400
What I want to explain
is why he's coming back

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.210
to MIT every three
years or so since 2010

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:06.300
to give us an update about
the state of AI and DeepMind.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.610
And the reason is
that in 2009, he

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:20.130
came to spend a few months as
a visiting postdoc in my lab,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.550
and then this went
pretty well, and as you

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:28.590
can see from this recommendation
that I wrote up there.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.910
And he applied to a Wellcome
Fellowship that he got,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.480
with the idea of then
spending the next five

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.900
years working between
London and Cambridge here.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.000
And fortunately, or
unfortunately, he

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.170
spent of these five years only
a few months with the Wellcome

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.840
Fellowship, but then
he started DeepMind.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.920
And this was 2010, I think.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:09.690
And so in 2011, we had here
actually the initial session

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.830
was exactly in this classroom.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:16.140
We had a symposium that George
Tenenbaum and I organized

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.100
by the name "Brains,
Minds, and Machines."

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.140
It was for the-- one
of the five symposia

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.320
for the 150 years
of MIT, in 2011.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.560
And it started with
a first session

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.810
in this classroom that was
titled "The Golden Age of AI".

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.490
And so you can see here
Sydney Brenner, Marvin Minsky,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.810
Noam Chomsky, and Emilio Betsy
and Patrick Winston at the end.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.450
And this was, by the way,
the first and only time

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.120
as far as I know that
Marvin Minsky and Noam

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.280
Chomsky were together on a
panel, which is pretty amazing.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.420
And this was the first day.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.560
The third day was "The
Marketplace for Intelligence".

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:05.580
And so there were the big
guns, like Microsoft and Google

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.700
and IBM speaking,
but there was also

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.370
startups, or other companies.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.860
I think Colby
spoke, Colby's here,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.790
and Amnon spoke, and Demis.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.860
Demis looked a
bit younger, but--

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:29.610
And so after that, I
asked him to become

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:34.680
a member of the Advisory
Committee of the Center

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.750
for Brains, Minds and Machines.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.620
Then we got with Josh
and several others

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:46.270
at MIT with funding
for NSF for 10 years.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:52.050
It's actually 2023 is the
last year of NSF funding

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:58.710
for the CBMM, and Demis has
been a great advisor together

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:02.790
with all the great advisors in
our external advisory committee

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.770
that will meet,
actually, tomorrow

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:08.340
to tell us what to do next.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.930
And so that's why
Demis is coming back.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.700
This is a little piece of his
bio that is not usually known.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.379
And Demis, I'm very glad
to give you the stage.

00:04:22.963 --> 00:04:23.880
DEMIS HASSABIS: Great.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.880
Thanks, Tommy, for that
great introduction.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.977
I stayed over with
Tommy on Sunday evening,

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:30.810
and he was saying that
he found in the attic

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.710
all of these ancient
documents of DeepMind from way

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.770
back in 2010-11 that
I completely forgot

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.510
about and lost track of, but
Tommy still had somewhere.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.950
So it was great reminiscing
about old times.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.440
So it's always fantastic
to be back here.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.400
It's been a few years now,
of course, with the pandemic,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.460
but it's always wonderful
to be back at MIT

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.090
and to talk to all of you.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.600
So today, what I'm
going to discuss

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.890
is mostly our latest
work, which is AlphaFold,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.660
and the impact it's had there.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.570
And hopefully, those of
you who are biologists

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.780
in the audience
have found it useful

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.910
yourselves and may be using it.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.860
It'd be great to
hear your feedback

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.360
on that in the Q&amp;A session.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.420
But before I talk about
AlphaFold specifically,

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.790
and actually a lot
of the other work

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.620
that we are using AI in the
various different sciences,

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.175
I'll briefly mentioned some
of the other work we've had.

00:05:28.175 --> 00:05:29.550
We've had a bit
of a golden year,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.370
I would say, last year
in terms of applying AI

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:37.380
to all sorts of hard
problems in the sciences.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.930
But I was going to
also just recapitulate

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.900
a few of the things that
have led up to being us

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.840
being able to do this.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.660
It's been a kind of 10 year--
well, I guess 12 years now--

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.230
process to get to this
point, where we're finally

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:53.730
at the point now,
I think, where we

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:59.350
can apply AI very meaningfully
to scientific discovery itself,

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.950
which was always my
aim with DeepMind

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.590
from the very beginning.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.830
So I just want to thread
the needle for you

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.160
in terms of how the different
work we've done in the past,

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.150
largely on games, then
culminated in the kind of work

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.910
that we're doing now.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:21.640
So as Tommy said, we
started DeepMind in 2010

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.600
with a very audacious vision.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.050
The plan was to effectively
build a kind of Apollo program

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.560
effort for building artificial
general intelligence,

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.530
or human level
artificial intelligence.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:39.790
And back in 2010, none of
you will remember this now,

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:45.100
but you know I was
uninvestable, I would say,

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.520
and certainly a
kind of backwater.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.320
And it's kind of
amazing to see what's

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.020
happened in a relatively
short space of time.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.420
Within a decade,
really, it's gone

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:58.210
from that to perhaps
one of the hottest

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:03.530
areas of technology, science,
and also commercial investing.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:04.760
So it's kind of amazing.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:09.100
And it's been a privilege to
be at the vanguard of that

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:13.930
and be part of pushing that
transformation forward.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:14.560
And in a way--

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.630
I mean, we always, of
course, plan for success,

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.820
but I think even
we've been pretty

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:23.050
surprised by how quickly
things have progressed

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:26.220
and where we've got to today.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.810
So the mission
statement of DeepMind

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.650
was to solve intelligence
and to use it to advance

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:35.840
science and benefit humanity.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.280
And we've had this in mind
from the very beginning.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.400
This has all sorts of
implications about what

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:41.780
we would think about.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.970
When we use the term solving
intelligence, what do we mean?

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.210
Well, you know, I've been
trained as a neuroscientist

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.500
and as a computer scientist.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.060
And really, DeepMind
in the early days

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.100
was an intersection,
really, of the best

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.040
ideas of from computer
science and AI

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.590
and machine learning
and the best ideas

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:04.160
that we had at that time
from systems neuroscience.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.720
And that's partly why I was
working in Tommy's group,

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.905
because obviously his group
does both of those two things.

00:08:11.905 --> 00:08:13.280
And by solving
intelligence, what

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.130
we mean is we'd like to
fundamentally understand

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.400
the phenomena of intelligence,
and then, of course,

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.970
recreate that
artificially to create

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:25.400
artificial general intelligence.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.350
And our thinking
was that if this

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.830
was done in a very general
way, then in theory,

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:33.799
one should be able to apply
the technologies that come out

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:36.230
of that, the algorithms that
come out of that process

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.830
to a whole wide range
of challenging tasks.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:42.890
After all, that is what the
human brain does, obviously,

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:44.930
our only existence proof
of general intelligence

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:47.090
that we have.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:50.003
And there's no, I don't
think, theoretical reason why

00:08:50.003 --> 00:08:51.170
that should not be possible.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.940
So the type of approach
we took at DeepMind,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.820
which was very early, if you
think about that in 2010,

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.410
and now, of course,
is all the rage,

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:03.710
is a learning systems approach.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.840
And it's turned out,
transpired, obviously,

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.570
over the last decade that
they're incredibly powerful

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.310
and they have huge
potential, these types

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:11.820
of learning systems.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.020
So of course, I think
of it as two components,

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.170
really, although there are
many types of learning systems.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.420
But of course, the one we
specialize in at DeepMind

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.440
is called deep
reinforcement learning.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.800
So one way you can think
about this, at least the way

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.320
I think about this is
if an agent or an AI

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.630
system on the left
here finds itself

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:32.840
in some kind of environment
or some data stream

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.350
and it's observing,
getting observations

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.870
of that data stream.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.780
And I think the first
job of the AI system

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:46.610
is to basically build a model of
that environment from that data

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:48.530
or that experience.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.560
And that could be a
continually updating thing

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.660
if this is an active
learning agent.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:57.690
So that's the first job of
the AI system, I would say.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.470
Then the second job, where
we use reinforcement learning

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.510
for this, but there are
other approaches, too,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:06.600
is once you have built that
model of the environment,

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.190
you can use that model to learn
how to achieve a specific goal

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.760
or maximize a reward,
usually specified

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.430
by the designers of the system,
within that environment.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.670
Reinforcement learning does
that by a sophisticated process

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:27.470
of trial and error in
order to figure out

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:31.490
what action to take that would
best get it towards its goal.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.610
And at least in an active
learning system or an agent

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:37.910
system, this is an
active learning process.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:42.230
So actions are taken that
may drive a new change

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.880
in the environment,
which in itself may

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.910
drive a new observation.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:49.850
So the cool thing about these
systems, deep RL systems,

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:52.910
is that they can discover
potentially new knowledge

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.550
from first principles
for themselves,

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:58.682
through this process
of trial and error.

00:10:58.682 --> 00:11:01.300
So of course, if one
thinks about that,

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:05.920
then this might be incredibly
powerful in certain domains,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.450
for example, like science.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.530
So obviously, as all of
you will probably know,

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:16.700
AlphaGo was our big
landmark success

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.730
with this type of
system, probably

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:21.200
still the most
famous demonstration

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.110
to date of a deep RL system.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:27.372
And just to remind those
of you who have not

00:11:27.372 --> 00:11:29.330
heard about that, of
course this is our program

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:31.280
to play the game of Go.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:35.600
And we know that Go was
super hard for computers

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.020
to play until AlphaGo came along
because of its enormous search

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.860
space and the fact that it's
a very esoteric game, so it's

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:47.030
very difficult to write down
a handcrafted evaluation

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:51.580
function in the way that
chess programs are written.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.880
And I think the reason this
is, is because Go is actually

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.410
at the edge of difficulty level
that even humans can cope with,

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:03.640
I would say, whereas chess is
probably just below that limit.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.970
It's slightly less
complex than Go.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:11.410
And I think Go players
deal with this complexity

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.710
by relying heavily on their
intuition and their pattern

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.620
matching skills rather than
their explicit calculation

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:22.035
skills, because it's just too
complicated for us to do that.

00:12:22.035 --> 00:12:23.410
And that's, of
course, the reason

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:27.130
why it's so difficult to program
these evaluation functions is

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.410
even the world's best
players would not

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:31.990
be able to describe to
you precisely how they

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:36.040
are evaluating a position
in terms of a bunch of rules

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:39.270
that could maybe be coded.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.400
So we built these
systems and then

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:45.360
the descendants of AlphaGo,
AlphaGo Zero, and then

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.060
AlphaZero and then
more recently MuZero,

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:52.680
and they all basically use
this underlying approach

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.320
that I'm going to describe
here, which is really more

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:59.340
the way the AlphaZero and
AlphaGo Zero worked, but you

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.010
can think of is the whole way
all of these types of systems

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:02.940
work.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.620
And it's pretty
simple to describe.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:08.280
So what happens is you
start with a neural network

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:12.690
that's basically random, and
it's trained through hundreds

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.660
of thousands and sometimes
millions of games of self-play

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:19.630
to get better at
evaluating a position,

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:23.910
whether it's good or not for
the opponent or for the agent,

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.640
and also to predict
what are the right--

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.370
the kind of most
likely moves that

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.880
should be made in a position
so that it can narrow down

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:36.010
this enormous search space.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:38.880
And so what happens is
you start with version 1,

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.780
let's say, of the
system and it's

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:45.210
going to be pretty bad,
almost random at playing.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:49.860
You do 100,000 self-play games.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.170
That generates a new
data set, and you

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:57.030
use that data set of games to
train a second neural network,

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:57.990
version 2.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.830
And version 2 tries to
predict which side will

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.500
win from a certain
position in those games,

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:09.480
and also what type of move,
try to guess which kind of move

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.070
is made by the v.01 system.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:15.590
So you train up v2, and
then you have a little mini

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:19.670
tournament between v.02
and v.01, 100 games or so.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.740
And there's some
kind of win threshold

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:24.950
where you feel like
v.02 needs to beat v.01

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:27.060
by a certain threshold.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.300
We set that 55% win rate.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:32.240
And then we regard that
as significantly stronger,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.430
the v.02 than the v.01.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:39.500
Now, if that happens and v.02
does beat v.01 55% or more

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:44.060
of the time, then you
replace v.01 with v.02

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:45.980
and you do this
whole cycle again.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:47.780
And you generate
a new set of data,

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:49.730
but this data will be
slightly higher quality,

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.520
because now it's v.02
that's generating that data,

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.725
and then you train
a version 3, right?

00:14:56.725 --> 00:14:58.850
And then you do the little
mini tournament, and you

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:00.320
and you and you go again.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:04.940
Now, if v.02 does not beat v.01
in that first mini tournament,

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.730
you continue to generate
more data with v.01.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:11.570
So you do another 100,000 games,
and now you've got 200,000

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:17.060
games to train the next
v.02*, one could call it.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:18.500
And then eventually,
what we find

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:21.230
is that v.02 does
eventually beat v.01.

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:25.820
And in fact, once one
does this around 17 times,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.550
depending on which game
it is, that kind of order,

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:31.970
17 to 20 times, you
go from random play

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:35.120
to better than human world
champion in any two player

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.370
game.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.560
And that's the system.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:41.260
So it's pretty elegant,
and we generalized

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:45.880
it fully to all two-player
games, not just Go,

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:50.103
but any two-player,
perfect information game.

00:15:50.103 --> 00:15:51.770
So this will become
relevant in a minute

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:53.750
when we discuss
about how to apply

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.310
these techniques to science.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:16:00.950
We think of Go really as when
you boil down what AlphaGo is,

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:04.130
Go is this enormous search
space, more positions

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:05.780
than there are atoms
in the universe,

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:08.990
impossible to exhaustively
search by brute force

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:11.900
or any brute force
method to find out

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:16.160
what the right path
through the Go game is.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:19.130
So what you can think of is
the neural net has learned

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:21.230
this model of the
environment, in this case,

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:26.090
Go positions and likely Go moves
and how good Go positions are.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:29.250
And that constrains this
enormous search space.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.420
So instead of having to search
this entire search space,

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:34.910
it just searches-- the
Monte Carlo tree search only

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:39.050
needs to search a small,
tiny fraction, this in blue,

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:41.120
of the possibilities.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:45.230
And then of those, of
course, it picks the best one

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:49.070
that comes out as the best
evaluation, this path in pink,

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.160
and that's the path that
is picked once the thinking

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.590
time runs out.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:58.340
And this program is obviously
enormously powerful.

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:00.350
We beat the 18-times
world champion

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:05.359
in a very famous match
in 2016 in Seoul,

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:07.579
which was watched by a couple
hundred million people.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:10.970
And we won that
match 4-1, and it

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.988
was proclaimed to be a
decade before its time.

00:17:12.988 --> 00:17:14.780
But those of you who
are familiar with this

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:16.730
will know that
was more important

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:20.990
was actually how AlphaGo
won that match, and the fact

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:24.619
that it came up with
original Go strategies

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:28.850
and Go motifs, including most
famously move 37 in Game 2,

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:31.580
which is here on the right
and encircled in red.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:35.510
And suffice to say
that this piece was

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.760
put on the fifth line, you
can see here the fifth line

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.110
from the edge of the board.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.240
And this early in the
game, you should never

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.550
play on the fifth line.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.050
We don't really
know why, but that's

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:49.280
the rules of Go
masters will tell you,

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:51.380
you should only play
a move like this

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:53.090
on the fourth line
or the third line,

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:55.220
but definitely not
the fifth line.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:58.220
Of course, that's all changed
now with AlphaGo doing this.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.130
And obviously, the subsequent
five-plus years now,

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:05.840
all these AlphaGo games
have been studied to death.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:08.180
And there are many, many
books now written about this,

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:10.842
and it's changed Go theory.

00:18:10.842 --> 00:18:12.800
And of course, the
interesting thing about this

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:18.950
was that this move 37 stone,
100 moves later, roughly,

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:22.340
was in exactly the right
position to decide the game,

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.650
because the fighting from the
bottom left of this position

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:27.500
spread across the
whole board, and then

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.800
the move 37 stone happened
to be in the perfect position

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:35.963
to decide that battle and
then win AlphaGo the game.

00:18:35.963 --> 00:18:37.130
So this is very interesting.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:40.185
So these are clearly
an original strategy,

00:18:40.185 --> 00:18:41.560
but we can come
back to, as well,

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:46.548
also in the Q&amp;A about what does
this mean about creativity.

00:18:46.548 --> 00:18:48.340
So maybe just to mention
a little bit about

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:51.130
that, I think AI systems
today are very good,

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:54.500
have been very good at for a
long time on interpolation.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.550
So you can think of that as
distinguishing between cats,

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.050
pictures of cats and dogs.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:02.800
And you show the system millions
of cats, millions of dogs,

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:04.180
and it kind of
effectively builds

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:07.390
a sort of prototype of what a
cat and what a dog looks like.

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:09.460
And then so that's kind
of like interpolation,

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:13.060
some kind of average of all
those examples and kind of

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:14.500
creates a template out of that.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:17.494
This, I would say, is
more like extrapolation.

00:19:17.494 --> 00:19:21.190
So these strategies
have never been played

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:23.170
before by human players, right?

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:25.540
So this is not
just interpolation,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.850
some kind of averaging
of what we know is good.

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:30.500
It's true, I would
say, extrapolation.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:33.220
So I think that is some
form of creativity,

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:34.480
one would have to say.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:37.240
But on the other hand, I
think there's a further level

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.760
to go, which I don't
think AI systems can do,

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.820
which one could call true
invention or out of the box

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:43.930
thinking.

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.600
And for me that would
be not coming up

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:51.250
with just an original strategy
in Go, but inventing Go, right?

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.260
So we don't have
any systems that

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:55.930
can invent Go or invent chess.

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:58.420
And that's a whole
other level, and it's

00:19:58.420 --> 00:19:59.920
interesting to
speculate, well, what

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:01.090
would be missing from that?

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:03.700
I think partially we wouldn't
know how to even describe that

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:08.230
to an AI system in a way
that isn't very high level

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:12.160
conceptual, and our current
systems don't really

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.520
deal with that very well.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:14.720
So I think there's one more.

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:16.870
So I think we're
starting to encroach

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:20.500
into the boundaries of
what on may call or think

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.650
of as creative, but
there's several more,

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:26.638
I think, levels still to go.

00:20:26.638 --> 00:20:28.430
So if you're interested
in this, of course,

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:30.388
there's the AlphaGo film
that's now on YouTube.

00:20:30.388 --> 00:20:34.270
And I think if you're
interested in the human story,

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:36.640
if you like, behind
AlphaGo, I highly

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:40.540
recommend this
award-winning documentary.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:42.710
So then with AlphaZero,
as I was mentioning,

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:46.040
we generalize this next to--
and this is, in general,

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:47.380
the way we tend to--

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:49.900
I think this is a good approach
to engineering sciences.

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.600
Usually, you-- at least
what we do at DeepMind

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.660
is you try and focus
on performance first.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:21:01.370
So you try and get can you
actually master Go at all.

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:04.360
Then once that performance
is reached, you throw out--

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:06.767
you start throwing out
components of the system

00:21:06.767 --> 00:21:08.350
to make it more and
more general, less

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:11.410
and less specific to
that particular task,

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:14.620
while still keeping that
performance level up, right?

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.810
And then finally, which
is the stage we're getting

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:20.290
to in certain of our systems,
like AlphaZero and a bit

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:23.410
AlphaFold, you can also do
analytics on that system

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:25.390
and try and understand
at a deep level,

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:29.890
open up the black box and
find out how, exactly,

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.950
it's working and what is the
knowledge that it's-- how is it

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:35.170
representing the
knowledge that it has.

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:39.250
So AlphaZero, amazingly,
within a few hours,

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:41.230
training from
nothing, would beat

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:45.868
the best programs available
in any of these other games.

00:21:45.868 --> 00:21:47.410
And these programs
are already better

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:49.700
than world champion, human
world champion level,

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.790
including, you'll see
at the bottom here,

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:54.590
AlphaGo, all the versions
of AlphaGo itself.

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:58.510
So AlphaZero would beat
all versions of AlphaGo.

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:01.270
And that's quite interesting
in itself, right?

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:03.940
So this idea of
generalization can also

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.400
give you a little bit
more performance, too,

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.630
is the lesson that we
also took from this,

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:10.570
which is quite an
interest-- maybe slightly

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:13.320
counterintuitive.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:15.720
Of course, because I'm
an ex chess player,

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:18.450
chess was always the
thing I was eventually

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:21.060
going to want to apply this to.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:23.880
And again, in chess, not only
was it exceptionally strong,

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.740
beating Stockfish 8,
which was at the time

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:28.050
the world champion
chess computer

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:30.870
of the traditional
type, but it sort of

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:35.070
invented a new style of chess
where it favored mobility

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.640
over materiality.

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:40.710
So almost all chess
programs until AlphaZero

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:42.060
favored material.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:46.590
So the classic cliche is
that computer grabs a pawn

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:49.710
because if it sees some
material that it can take,

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.770
it will try and grab it even
if it then has to hang on

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:54.845
for dear life and defend.

00:22:54.845 --> 00:22:56.220
And of course,
computers are very

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:57.887
good at defending
positions because they

00:22:57.887 --> 00:23:00.653
don't make any mistakes,
tactical mistakes.

00:23:00.653 --> 00:23:02.820
But this was always considered
to be a very ugly way

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:03.780
of playing chess.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:06.000
So no one would
say, oh, we've seen

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.970
this wonderful beautiful
chess computer game.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:09.660
They were ugly.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:11.100
They were effective but ugly.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.520
And so this is what
changed about AlphaZero

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.430
is not only was it better,
they were beautiful games,

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:20.250
or at least considered to
be beautiful, like this game

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.350
here, where AlphaZero is white.

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:25.290
And you can see
what it's done is

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:28.140
it sacrificed a lot
of pieces in exchange

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:31.950
and some pawns in order
to totally imprison

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:34.980
the black pieces and block
all the black pieces in.

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:36.510
So in fact, none
of the Black pieces

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:38.730
can move without
losing something,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:42.270
even though the black is
winning on material here.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:44.550
And in chess, that's
called Zugzwang,

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:49.260
that kind of position
where any move you make

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.610
makes your position worse.

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:53.550
And this kind of position
was never seen before.

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.290
The famous YouTube
chess commentators

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.380
called this the
immortal Zugzwang game

00:23:57.380 --> 00:24:00.060
because no one had ever seen
a position like this, where

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:03.330
you've got such powerful pieces
like the Queens and the Rooks

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:06.190
and they can't do anything.

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:08.220
So this is pretty incredible.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:10.140
How is AlphaZero doing this?

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.200
Well, my speculation, and
we've also analyzed it

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.950
with some of the
ex-world chess champions,

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:17.910
and we opened up the system to
them, like Vladimir Kramnik,

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:20.280
and they helped us
understand that.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:23.400
And we've written a
paper recently analyzing,

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.610
seeing if we can reverse
engineer AlphaZero and find out

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:31.200
what the rules are that it's
built implicitly in AlphaZero.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:33.300
And it's obviously better
at evaluating positions

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:34.140
than chess engines.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:35.640
And the reason is
because it doesn't

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.140
have to overcome
these inbuilt rules.

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:41.460
So in order-- if you think
about why chess computers don't

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.650
sacrifice their
pieces a lot, it's

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:47.310
because they have inbuilt
tables, valuation tables that

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:49.320
tell them Rooks
are worth 5 points,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:50.800
Knights are worth 3 points.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:53.970
So in order for it to
sacrifice a Rook for a Knight,

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:56.010
it will have to
work out explicitly

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:57.780
through its brute
force search that it's

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:00.670
going to get the 2 points
back somehow, right?

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:03.750
And if it can't work that
out within its event horizon,

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.600
then it won't do it,
whereas AlphaZero doesn't

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.210
have those input rules,
so it's much freer

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.180
to take into account
the contextual factors

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.730
of the current position and
just say in this position,

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.200
that Knight is super powerful.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:17.565
It's on an outpost.

00:25:17.565 --> 00:25:20.040
And my Rook's passive, so
I'm just going to swap them.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:24.600
It doesn't have to overcome
this inbuilt bias, if you like.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:27.030
It's also better at
balancing different factors

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:27.903
against each other.

00:25:27.903 --> 00:25:29.820
So one of the problems
with rule-based systems

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:33.000
is that you have maybe 1,000
rules at this point in the best

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.550
chess programs, and not
only are those rules

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:39.540
are they the right rules and
are they expressed correctly,

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:43.180
but also one has to balance
those rules against each other,

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:47.640
which is a super difficult kind
of hand optimization problem.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:50.670
And also, probably, you'd
want a different balance

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:52.350
in different positions.

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:55.260
And of course, a chess
computer, rules-based computer

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:56.730
is not able to do that, right?

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.090
You have to have one
set of balancing rules.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:03.300
But AlphaZero, again, is free
to perhaps overweight things

00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:07.433
dynamically, depending
on the position.

00:26:07.433 --> 00:26:09.100
The other cool thing
about these systems

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:15.550
is they do a lot less search
for the strength of the play.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.880
So a human
grandmaster, our models

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.683
are incredibly
sophisticated so we only

00:26:21.683 --> 00:26:23.350
have to do a very
small amount of search

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:24.700
to come up with a good move.

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:28.090
So maybe a human grandmaster
does maybe a few hundred moves

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:30.370
of search for every decision.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.740
State of the art chess
engines, these brute force

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:34.900
rules-based engines need
to do tens of millions

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:39.562
of searches per
evaluate, per decision.

00:26:39.562 --> 00:26:41.270
And AlphaZero is
somewhere in the middle.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.690
It's not quite as efficient
yet as human grandmasters,

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:45.850
but it's orders of
magnitude more efficient

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:48.705
than standard chess programs.

00:26:48.705 --> 00:26:50.830
So if you're interested in
reading more about this,

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:52.372
there's obviously
our paper, and also

00:26:52.372 --> 00:26:55.960
a whole book written by
the British chess champion

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:59.770
about all the different
styles and the implications

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.650
of that for chess that
AlphaZero came up with.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:05.260
And in fact, you don't
have to just trust

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:06.520
my description of that.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:10.840
Here's a couple of really nice
quotes from Magnus Carlson,

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:12.970
who's an amazing world
champ, the current world

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:14.920
champion, amazing chess player.

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:17.860
"I have been influenced by my
heroes recently, one of which

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:18.970
is AlphaZero."

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:20.740
And I think he was the
first player really

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:24.790
to integrate these new
ideas into his own play.

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:28.930
And Kasparov said,
"Programs usually reflect

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:31.510
the priorities and prejudices
of the programmers,

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:33.490
but because AlphaZero
learns for itself,

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.550
I would say its style reflects
the truth," which is a pretty

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:39.710
cool thing for him to say.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:41.480
So just wrapping
this up, then, we've

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.150
been very fortunate
to make a whole bunch

00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:49.880
of big breakthroughs in game
AI, starting with our Atari work

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:52.880
back in 2012, actually,
and published in 2014,

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:57.050
where learning to play directly
from the pixels on the screen

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:59.120
and no other information,
AlphaGo and AlphaZero,

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.480
I just mentioned to you.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.170
And then finally, we
tackled the most what's

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:06.050
considered to be the most
complex strategy game video

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:14.150
game, StarCraft II, and reached
a grandmaster level at that,

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:16.580
as well, with AlphaStar.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:21.170
So that was really the history
of where we've been working on,

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:24.590
on games, because they're
so efficient to develop

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:28.820
these algorithmic ideas,
they're really easy to test.

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:30.832
They're safe
sandboxes, in a way.

00:28:30.832 --> 00:28:32.540
Like, it doesn't really
matter if your AI

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:33.750
does something wrong.

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:35.250
It's just a game.

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:36.860
But also, they are
really good metrics

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:39.410
to measure how are
you progressing.

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:42.080
Obviously, the win rate
or the grading, or you

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:46.040
can evaluate the quality
of the move or benchmark

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:48.170
it against the best human
players in the world

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:51.050
in each one of these games.

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:53.600
So the exciting
thing was actually

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:56.930
our shift in the last
few years to start

00:28:56.930 --> 00:29:01.250
to use these AI systems to
explore scientific discovery

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:02.240
itself.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:07.670
And what we looked for is three
criteria for a suitable problem

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:11.430
that maybe we can tackle
with these types of systems.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:16.400
So first of all, we actually
actively seek out problems

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.220
that can be considered as
massive combinatorial search

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:21.410
spaces or state spaces.

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:26.720
And the reason we like
those types of problems

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:31.490
is that means that exhaustive
brute force approaches will not

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:32.900
be tractable here, right?

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:37.760
So we actively seek out
these types of regimes.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:40.227
And we think we're
particularly suitable--

00:29:40.227 --> 00:29:42.560
our techniques are particularly
suitable for these types

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:43.700
of regimes.

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:47.300
Second of all, we look for
a clear objective function

00:29:47.300 --> 00:29:51.620
or measure to optimize
against, so that we can hill

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:55.730
climb against it and figure
out if we're making progress

00:29:55.730 --> 00:29:56.970
or not.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:29:59.930
And then finally, we
look for obviously we

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:02.540
need enough data in which
to learn our models,

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:06.260
and/or potentially the existence
of an accurate and efficient

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:07.930
simulator.

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:11.290
And-- ideally, it's an and--

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:13.240
and in most of the
things that we tackle,

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.493
we usually have both.

00:30:15.493 --> 00:30:17.160
It's very interesting,
actually, though,

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:18.930
that one of the
things that we've

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:22.050
got very good at over the
years we've been doing this

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:26.100
is that even if you don't
have enough data to learn

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:29.730
your model directly, you may
have enough data in order

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:32.670
to cobble together a simulator.

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:35.580
And then the simulator
can then be run,

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:39.240
and to generate synthetic
data, more synthetic data

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.820
to augment your real data.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:43.440
And you can also
analyze that data

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:46.110
to see if it's from the right
distribution and the quality

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:46.650
of it.

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:49.530
And we've actually found that
even if the simulator data is

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:53.370
of less-- usually, it's not as
good quality as the real data.

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.590
As long as there's
some signal in it,

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:58.140
it's actually still
pretty useful.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:01.320
And we actually used a
little bit of a variation

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:04.320
on this technique for
AlphaFold, so it was actually

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:06.600
an important part
of AlphaFold, which

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:10.260
I'll come back to in a minute.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:14.480
So those are the three
criteria, if you like.

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:17.060
And it turns out
if you use this,

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:19.090
so you can use this
criteria to look

00:31:19.090 --> 00:31:21.520
at what would be
useful commercially,

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:26.560
and we now have hundreds
of products within Google

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:29.050
that use DeepMind technology.

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:33.220
So pretty much
anything, any service

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:36.730
that you use from Google will
have some DeepMind technology

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:39.610
kind of under the hood
there, whether that's

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:44.050
YouTube recommendations to
your battery life saving

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:47.320
on your Android phones.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:49.750
Or if you speak
to a Google device

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:53.360
and it speaks back to you,
the text to speech systems

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:56.930
are our system powering
that underneath.

00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:59.260
And so you can look
into commercial areas

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:02.830
to see what fits these criteria,
and also, interestingly,

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:03.820
scientifically.

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:06.670
And it turns out if you do
that, there are actually

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:13.512
a lot of things that fit very
well with this set of criteria.

00:32:13.512 --> 00:32:14.970
And of course, the
number one thing

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:17.550
that we try to do that
ticks all of these boxes

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:20.370
is the protein folding problem.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:22.590
I'm sure many of you
will know what this is,

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:25.260
but for those that
don't, it's the problem

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.820
of going from an
amino acid sequence,

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:31.890
so the genetic sequence that
describes a protein, directly

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:35.550
to its final 3D
structure that it attains

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:37.910
in nature or in the body.

00:32:37.910 --> 00:32:39.860
So this is super useful.

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:40.720
Why is it useful?

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:44.110
Because proteins themselves
are essential to life.

00:32:44.110 --> 00:32:48.100
All of life's mechanisms
depend on proteins.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:50.140
And their 3D
structure is thought

00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:53.590
to at least partially determine
their function, right?

00:32:53.590 --> 00:32:55.990
The 3D structure of
the protein tells you

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:58.210
quite a lot about its function.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:01.570
And also, if you want to do
things like drug discovery,

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:04.580
you generally want to target
the surface of the protein,

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:07.390
so you need to know
its 3D shape in order

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:11.020
to know what the right target
areas are for you to target

00:33:11.020 --> 00:33:12.785
your chemical compound against.

00:33:14.770 --> 00:33:16.930
So this is the protein
folding problem.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:19.270
But the problem is that
determining the structure

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:23.920
experimentally usually
takes years of painstaking

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:26.260
experimental work.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:27.820
I think the normal
rule of thumb is

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:30.700
that it takes one PhD
student their entire PhD

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:36.700
to do one protein, and
oftentimes it doesn't work out.

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:42.320
And it's very difficult,
painstaking, technical work.

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:47.360
Now famously, Christian
Anfinsen, about 50 years ago,

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:50.900
actually, almost to the day,
during his Nobel lecture

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:52.910
acceptance speech, he--

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:55.130
it's almost a bit like
a Fermat's last theorem

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:57.500
situation, where he
had a throwaway comment

00:33:57.500 --> 00:33:59.720
in a way of like, well,
yes the 3D structure

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:02.060
of a protein should
be fully determined

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:03.510
by its amino acid sequence.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:06.860
So in theory, this
should be possible,

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:08.570
but who knows how
to do it, right?

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:12.920
So this sparked off a whole
50-year grand challenge,

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:18.650
or quest in biology to try and
solve this problem of protein

00:34:18.650 --> 00:34:22.010
folding, and going directly
from this amino acid sequence

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:25.070
to the 3D structure.

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:28.460
Now, one of his contemporaries,
Levinthal, at this point

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:31.969
formulated what's known
as Levinthal's paradox.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:34.670
And the question is, the big
question for protein folding

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:36.679
is can the protein
structure prediction

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:40.130
problem be solved
computationally, right, purely

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:41.330
computationally?

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:43.440
And one of the issues
is Levinthal's paradox,

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:45.199
which is he worked
out back of envelope,

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:46.880
estimated that
there were about 10

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:50.989
to the power 300 possible
shapes a protein could take,

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:53.989
an average size
protein could take.

00:34:53.989 --> 00:34:56.840
So clearly, exhaustively
sampling this

00:34:56.840 --> 00:34:58.700
is totally out of the question.

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:00.830
And yet obviously, the
paradox part of this

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:03.030
is that in nature
and in our bodies,

00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:06.920
these proteins spontaneously
fold, sometimes

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:08.610
in milliseconds.

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:14.870
So obviously, nature somehow
solves this very efficiently,

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:18.650
right, in an energetic sense.

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:20.150
So that points to
the fact that this

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:24.440
may be possible if we could
attack it in the right way.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:27.160
And for me, this has been
a very long road, actually,

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:29.710
to get into protein folding.

00:35:29.710 --> 00:35:32.740
I first came across
the problem in the '90s

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:34.690
as a student in Cambridge.

00:35:34.690 --> 00:35:38.470
One of my friends
circle there was

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:41.380
obsessed with this problem,
the protein folding problem,

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:42.310
even back then.

00:35:42.310 --> 00:35:46.030
And I remember at any
opportunity in the bars

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:48.970
and playing pool and
in the college bars

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:53.440
that he would talk about this
problem being foundational,

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:55.540
like if we could crack
this problem then

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:58.720
it would open up all sorts
of new avenues for biology.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:01.780
And that really stuck in my mind
as he described the problem,

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.645
and I thought it was
a fascinating problem.

00:36:03.645 --> 00:36:05.020
And I also in the
back of my mind

00:36:05.020 --> 00:36:08.950
felt it was the type of problem
that AI might one day be

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:10.870
suitable for AI to tackle.

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:12.820
So I kind of filed it
away, because obviously

00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:14.560
the AI techniques
back in the '90s

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:16.420
is when I was still
writing computer games

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:19.930
and doing my AI in the
guise of computer games

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:23.500
was not ready to tackle anything
remotely like protein folding,

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:27.370
but it really stuck to me
as a fascinating problem.

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.620
And then the second time I
came across it was actually

00:36:29.620 --> 00:36:34.780
when I was doing my postdoc
with Tommy here at MIT,

00:36:34.780 --> 00:36:37.810
and this game came
out called Foldit.

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:39.850
Some of you might remember.

00:36:39.850 --> 00:36:42.760
It came out, I think in
roughly 2008 from Baker Lab,

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:46.660
and it was really, I think,
the best and probably still

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:49.540
the best example of what one
would call a citizen science

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:50.050
game.

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:51.820
So what happened with
this game, Foldit,

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:56.110
is that the Baker Lab turned
the protein folding problem

00:36:56.110 --> 00:37:00.010
into a puzzle game, pretty
esoteric but quite fun

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:02.170
puzzle game for of
that persuasion.

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:06.040
And they actually got
lots and lots of gamers

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.200
to play this game
and actually choose

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:13.760
to make bends in the
backbone of the protein.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:17.980
And you get an estimate of the
energy score, right, basically.

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:21.640
And you were trying to
minimize this free energy.

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:23.658
And what was really cool
about that is that--

00:37:23.658 --> 00:37:25.450
well, firstly, I was
fascinated by the idea

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.730
of the general public
having fun with the game,

00:37:27.730 --> 00:37:29.140
but really they
were contributing

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:31.990
to science, which seems
like if we could actually

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:34.960
tap that, that would be
pretty cool for gamers

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:36.970
and for science, I think.

00:37:36.970 --> 00:37:39.910
And so I was pretty
fascinated by that idea.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:43.180
But also, I was quite
intrigued that the best players

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:45.880
of the Foldit game
actually found

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:48.910
some real structures, and
actually apparently some pretty

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:51.430
important ones that were
published in Nature and Nature

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:56.080
Structural Molecular Biology
in the subsequent years.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.140
And so I was thinking as I
was watching this, looking

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:01.690
at this in 2010, roughly
that kind of time,

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:04.340
well, what's happened there?

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:05.690
How is this possible?

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:09.940
And when you look into the kind
of moves that the players made,

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:13.660
they were making local moves
that were actually increasing

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:15.580
the energy, the free
energy for the moment,

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:18.070
because overall they
realized later on they

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:21.280
might be able to solve that--

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:23.980
actually globally, that
there would be lower energy.

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:29.440
And this is the reason why
greedy energy minimization

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.720
methods don't work,
because they just

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:33.925
get stuck into local maxima.

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:40.705
So they're obviously able to
somehow use their intuition,

00:38:40.705 --> 00:38:42.580
I guess the pattern
matching of these gamers.

00:38:42.580 --> 00:38:45.712
They weren't biologists,
they were just gamers.

00:38:45.712 --> 00:38:47.920
But some of them are really
good at pattern matching,

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:51.760
and they presumably were
using that to find their way,

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:55.810
their intuition, if you like, to
find their way to a good fold.

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:58.990
So then later on-- so
that was filed away, too.

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:04.270
And then later on, when we beat
Lee Sedol in the Seoul match

00:39:04.270 --> 00:39:07.780
and I thought back to in
the process of that what

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:09.400
were we doing,
really, with AlphaGo

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:13.600
is we mimicked the intuition
of these Go masters, right?

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:16.648
And believe me, if you talked
to these Go masters, that's

00:39:16.648 --> 00:39:18.190
all they've ever
done in their lives.

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:21.070
I play Go from the age of
two or three years old,

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:22.125
go to Go school--

00:39:22.125 --> 00:39:23.500
they don't go to
a normal school.

00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:27.340
That's-- you cannot get a
more highly trained mind

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:30.790
for a particular task
than a Go master.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:33.110
I literally-- I think
it's kind of impossible.

00:39:33.110 --> 00:39:36.640
So if we were able to mimic
their intuition in Go,

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:38.800
then why would it
not be possible to do

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:41.290
something analogous
in this protein space?

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:44.560
So that gave me the feeling
that this was definitely

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:48.190
a crackable problem
with our techniques.

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:51.220
So we actually started
the AlphaFold project

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:53.800
almost the day after we
got back off the plane

00:39:53.800 --> 00:39:55.900
from Seoul with AlphaGo.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:58.000
And we actually found
a bit of recording

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.940
somewhere where I was talking to
Dave Silver, who is the project

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:03.250
leader now for Go
out in Seoul, and I

00:40:03.250 --> 00:40:05.668
think we must have just won
the third match or something.

00:40:05.668 --> 00:40:07.210
And we were literally
discussing this

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:10.300
like I think now is the
time to push forward

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:12.670
on the protein folding project.

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:15.190
So that's what we did.

00:40:15.190 --> 00:40:18.790
Another really important
part of the story

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.340
was the existence
of this competition

00:40:21.340 --> 00:40:24.560
called CASP, Critical
Assessment of protein Structure

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:25.370
Prediction.

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:27.590
And it's an amazing competition.

00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:30.980
It's considered to be like the
Olympics for protein folding,

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:32.990
and it runs every two years.

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:35.870
And it's run by this amazing
group of organizers who've

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:37.710
been running it since 1994.

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.420
So they've diligently kept this
going for close to 30 years

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:42.030
now.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:46.970
And what they do is
that throughout a year,

00:40:46.970 --> 00:40:49.550
experimental departments
produce new crystals

00:40:49.550 --> 00:40:54.170
and experimental determined
structures of proteins, right?

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:56.510
But over a kind of three
month period, what they do

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.660
is they hold back
publishing those results

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:03.440
and they instead submit their
newly freshly found structures

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:05.300
into this competition.

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:07.400
But it's a blind
assessment, so the people,

00:41:07.400 --> 00:41:12.350
the computational teams that
compete in this competition

00:41:12.350 --> 00:41:14.660
don't know the structure.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:16.220
Nobody, in fact,
knows the structure

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:19.130
other than the group, the
experimental group that

00:41:19.130 --> 00:41:20.720
had just found it.

00:41:20.720 --> 00:41:22.580
And then you get--
it's quite exciting.

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:25.460
You get a sequence
emailed to you

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:27.710
and you have one week to
get back with your best

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:29.450
guess of the structure.

00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:32.780
And it's pretty fun, and
you do it over a summer.

00:41:32.780 --> 00:41:35.362
And then at the end of
all that, the organizers,

00:41:35.362 --> 00:41:37.070
they release the real
structures and they

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:40.970
measure how close were
your predictor structures

00:41:40.970 --> 00:41:44.750
of all the different teams
to the experimentally

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:47.980
determined ground truth.

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:50.160
And so I want to
give a big shout out

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:52.440
to John Moult, who is
the founder of this

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:54.570
and the other CASP
organizers, who

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:56.550
work so hard over
many, many years

00:41:56.550 --> 00:41:59.010
to maintain this competition.

00:41:59.010 --> 00:42:03.780
And I actually think it's a
pretty good model for helping

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:05.790
science to progress, and
maybe should be thought

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:08.280
about in other domains, too.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:11.310
So historically, then, what
have been happening at CASP?

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:13.920
Well, it turns out when
you look back on it

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.970
that there'd been very
little progress at CASP

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:20.360
for over a decade.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:25.240
So here on this graph, I'm
showing you the winning--

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:28.210
the median accuracy
for the winning team

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:30.910
at each subsequent
CASP competition,

00:42:30.910 --> 00:42:36.040
starting in 2006 with CASP 7
and going to 2016 with CASP 12.

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:40.600
And on the y-axis, you can see
it's a measure of the accuracy

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:44.942
that the winning team
got, and it's called GDT.

00:42:44.942 --> 00:42:46.900
I won't go into details
of how it's calculated,

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:50.080
but you can think about,
roughly speaking, it's

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:53.290
the percentage of residues
that you have accurately

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:56.270
put in the right position
within a certain tolerance,

00:42:56.270 --> 00:42:58.540
certain distance
tolerance, right?

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:03.340
So obviously, you're trying to
get close to 100% if you can.

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:05.230
And you can see here
that basically there'd

00:43:05.230 --> 00:43:06.910
been almost no
progress, and they

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:10.270
were stuck around this
40% level that's basically

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:12.080
a kind of useless structure.

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.560
So this was sort of stagnated.

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:17.382
And then we came
along in 2018, which

00:43:17.382 --> 00:43:19.090
is the first one we
entered after working

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:21.580
on AlphaFold for a couple
of years with AlphaFold 1,

00:43:21.580 --> 00:43:23.930
and we made this huge leap.

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:26.325
And I think it kind of
revolutionized the field.

00:43:26.325 --> 00:43:28.450
So we were the first-- it
was the first time really

00:43:28.450 --> 00:43:31.390
that cutting edge machine
learning had been applied

00:43:31.390 --> 00:43:36.130
at scale as the core
component of a solution

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:38.580
to the protein folding problem.

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:40.510
And AlphaFold 1 won
the competition.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:43.780
But not only did it win,
it won by almost 50%

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:46.810
above the next best team.

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:48.700
And you can see,
so that would have

00:43:48.700 --> 00:43:52.780
meant that the teams were still
tracking along the gray bars

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:56.758
if it wasn't for
this new advance.

00:43:56.758 --> 00:43:59.050
And then of course, that's
not where we wanted to stop.

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:02.180
We wanted to actually
solve the problem.

00:44:02.180 --> 00:44:04.480
And we had-- in
2020, we actually

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:07.630
re-architected the whole
system for the 2020 competition

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:10.480
to reach atomic accuracy.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:11.950
And that was AlphaFold 2.

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:19.870
So that was, again, another even
bigger leap above AlphaFold 1.

00:44:19.870 --> 00:44:23.050
So these were the results
in terms of accuracy.

00:44:23.050 --> 00:44:26.530
So AlphaFold 2 sort of
achieved this atomic accuracy

00:44:26.530 --> 00:44:27.940
at CASP 14.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:30.970
And we were told
by the organizers

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:35.710
back in 2016-17 that the magic
threshold that they considered

00:44:35.710 --> 00:44:38.110
to be the threshold that
needed to be crossed

00:44:38.110 --> 00:44:41.140
was less than 1 Angstrom error.

00:44:41.140 --> 00:44:43.030
That was the kind of
critical threshold

00:44:43.030 --> 00:44:45.730
for it to be competitive
with experimental methods,

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:47.510
and therefore
useful in practice.

00:44:47.510 --> 00:44:49.340
So that's what we were
always aiming for,

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:52.000
so less than 1 angstrom, so
atomic accuracy, basically

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:54.670
the width of a carbon atom.

00:44:54.670 --> 00:44:57.040
And AlphaFold 2
crossed that line

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.080
and actually achieve
a 0.96 Angstrom

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:03.280
error in the CASP 14 competition
for the first time ever.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:06.880
And you can see this was still
actually three times more

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:09.610
accurate than the
next best systems

00:45:09.610 --> 00:45:13.990
from some commercial ones like
from Tencent, but also the best

00:45:13.990 --> 00:45:16.600
labs that work in this area,
Baker Labs, Zhang Lab, and so

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:20.380
on, which were still around the
3 Angstrom error in CASP 14,

00:45:20.380 --> 00:45:23.380
even though they'd already
incorporated the AlphaFold 1

00:45:23.380 --> 00:45:29.200
advances that obviously we'd
published after CASP 13.

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:32.065
So this led to the
organizers and John Moult

00:45:32.065 --> 00:45:34.600
and the organizers declaring
the structure prediction

00:45:34.600 --> 00:45:38.230
problem, protein folding
essentially solved.

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:43.775
And here's some examples of
the outputs of AlphaFold.

00:45:43.775 --> 00:45:45.400
Many of you will have
seen this by now,

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:48.730
but this was actually
incredibly wonderful

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:51.280
for us to see this
when we got given back

00:45:51.280 --> 00:45:53.710
the results from CASP 14.

00:45:53.710 --> 00:45:56.440
And you can see how good
our predictions are,

00:45:56.440 --> 00:45:59.230
the ones in blue, and the
ground truth is in green.

00:45:59.230 --> 00:46:00.950
And they're overlaid
over each other,

00:46:00.950 --> 00:46:04.450
and you can see they're
highly overlapping,

00:46:04.450 --> 00:46:08.290
including on the left hand side,
this is a protein from SARS

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:11.860
that even the side chain, so
these little thin stalks which

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:14.650
are the side chains, not on
the backbone of the protein,

00:46:14.650 --> 00:46:16.315
are accurately predicted, too.

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:24.420
And this is the architecture
for AlphaFold 2.

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:27.420
So there's a whole
bunch of innovations

00:46:27.420 --> 00:46:29.230
that were required here.

00:46:29.230 --> 00:46:31.080
I haven't got time to
go into all of them,

00:46:31.080 --> 00:46:33.390
but I'll give you the
kind of key takeaways.

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:37.170
If you're interested
in the details there,

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:39.690
you can check out
our Nature paper

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:42.870
that has about 60 pages of
supplemental information.

00:46:42.870 --> 00:46:48.150
So it's maybe just a measure
of how complex this system was.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:51.510
And it was by far the most
complicated system that we

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:53.580
have ever built at DeepMind.

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:55.680
And we built some pretty
complicated systems,

00:46:55.680 --> 00:46:58.860
but this is, I would say,
the most complex one ever.

00:46:58.860 --> 00:47:01.290
There were actually 32
component algorithms

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:05.370
that went into AlphaFold, as I
mentioned, the 60 pages of SI.

00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:08.040
And there was no
real silver bullet

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:10.230
in the end that solved
protein folding.

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:13.080
It actually needed a whole host
of insights and innovations,

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:15.960
and then those innovations
had to be carefully integrated

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:17.070
together.

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:20.730
But the key takeaways were, and
the key advances of AlphaFold 2

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:23.850
compared to AlphaFold
1 was that, firstly, it

00:47:23.850 --> 00:47:27.240
was an end to end system with
a kind of iterative recycling

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:31.290
stage that actually builds
the structure piece by piece

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:32.520
and builds that out.

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:34.350
And that allowed
us to go directly

00:47:34.350 --> 00:47:39.780
from the amino acid sequence
directly to the 3D structure.

00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:43.030
With AlphaFold 1, there
was an intermediate step,

00:47:43.030 --> 00:47:46.170
which is that we went from
the amino acid sequence

00:47:46.170 --> 00:47:50.010
to what's called a distogram,
so a pairwise distogram of all

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:51.990
the residues to
all the residues.

00:47:51.990 --> 00:47:55.080
And then there was a
kind of Newton method

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:58.473
to optimize that, a numerical
method that actually

00:47:58.473 --> 00:47:59.640
took the longest part of it.

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:03.030
It took like one
week per protein.

00:48:03.030 --> 00:48:08.445
And both those two systems had
to be optimized separately.

00:48:08.445 --> 00:48:10.320
And those of you who
work on machine learning

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:12.210
will know, be
familiar with the idea

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:14.220
that the more end to
end you can make things,

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:15.750
generally the better it is.

00:48:15.750 --> 00:48:18.528
The kind of optimization that
you do seems to flow better.

00:48:18.528 --> 00:48:20.070
And that was definitely
the case here

00:48:20.070 --> 00:48:24.213
when we were able to optimize
directly for the 3D structure.

00:48:24.213 --> 00:48:26.630
We also used an attention-based
neural network rather than

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:28.070
convolutional neural network.

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:30.230
And that was key
because we were using

00:48:30.230 --> 00:48:32.808
a convolutional neural
network for AlphaFold 1.

00:48:32.808 --> 00:48:34.350
And of course, if
you think about it,

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:36.000
that's the wrong
type of bias, right?

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:39.920
Like obviously, pixels on
an image, if they're nearby,

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:42.360
they're pretty correlated
in their properties.

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:44.990
But actually, in
fact, that's not

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:48.728
true at all, necessarily,
of amino acids that

00:48:48.728 --> 00:48:50.520
are near each other in
the sequence, right?

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:53.990
Obviously, it completely
depends on how the backbone

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:56.780
is going to bend round.

00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:58.820
So this attention-based
neural network

00:48:58.820 --> 00:49:00.980
would infer the implicit
graph structure.

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:03.230
And then finally, we
were able to build in--

00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:06.230
and this was critical--
evolutionary and physical

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:09.590
constraints into the
model architecture

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:12.620
without impacting the training
and the learning, right?

00:49:12.620 --> 00:49:16.700
So that was pretty difficult
to build in these constraints

00:49:16.700 --> 00:49:18.980
without hurting the learning.

00:49:18.980 --> 00:49:22.370
But in the end, that turned
out to be totally essential.

00:49:22.370 --> 00:49:25.120
So it was a kind of
huge research effort,

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:28.420
took over five years, at
its peak, about 20 people,

00:49:28.420 --> 00:49:30.590
highly multidisciplinary team.

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:33.107
So we have lots of
biologists and chemists

00:49:33.107 --> 00:49:34.690
and physicists on
the team, as well as

00:49:34.690 --> 00:49:37.570
obviously machine
learning and engineering.

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:40.180
And the other thing to
note is if the output

00:49:40.180 --> 00:49:46.480
of a particular applied system
is in itself really valuable

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:48.790
like we thought this
would be for biologists,

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:51.790
then one, maybe you should
relax the constraint

00:49:51.790 --> 00:49:53.020
of being general.

00:49:53.020 --> 00:49:55.660
So normally, with all our
machine learning research,

00:49:55.660 --> 00:49:57.400
we try and be as
general as possible.

00:49:57.400 --> 00:50:01.760
But here, we wanted to just
solve the problem by any means.

00:50:01.760 --> 00:50:04.780
So we used all of our
general purpose knowledge

00:50:04.780 --> 00:50:06.820
and general purpose
algorithms, but then

00:50:06.820 --> 00:50:09.460
we actually built in quite
a lot of domain knowledge

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:11.560
in this case, like the
evolutionary physical

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:13.690
constraints, because
we just wanted

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:17.300
to solve the problem here.

00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:20.000
In terms of speed and
scale, actually, the system

00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:23.840
turns out to be incredibly
fast, especially inference.

00:50:23.840 --> 00:50:27.770
So it takes about two weeks
on 8 GPUs, roughly 150 GPUs

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:31.850
to train, so actually a fairly
modest amount of compute

00:50:31.850 --> 00:50:33.620
by today's standards.

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:36.410
And the inference
is super fast, so

00:50:36.410 --> 00:50:40.550
order of minutes for an average
protein on a single GPU.

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:42.260
So we were thinking
about how to give

00:50:42.260 --> 00:50:46.940
broad access to the biology
community using the system.

00:50:46.940 --> 00:50:50.120
And normally what you
do with these systems

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:52.340
as you put them on
a server, and then

00:50:52.340 --> 00:50:54.620
people submit their
amino acid sequences

00:50:54.620 --> 00:50:56.450
and you give them back
a structure, right?

00:50:56.450 --> 00:50:57.825
But actually, we
thought we could

00:50:57.825 --> 00:51:00.710
do a lot better than that
here and just fold everything

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:02.822
for everyone immediately.

00:51:02.822 --> 00:51:05.030
And we wanted-- so we thought,
OK, what should we do?

00:51:05.030 --> 00:51:08.870
Because it's fast enough, let's
start with the human proteome.

00:51:08.870 --> 00:51:11.240
So that's what we did, and
we actually folded this

00:51:11.240 --> 00:51:15.140
over Christmas, I guess, 2020.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:17.900
So we had this idea straight
off the competition,

00:51:17.900 --> 00:51:20.120
lots of computers are
sitting idle over Christmas,

00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:23.270
so we just set them going
on the human proteome

00:51:23.270 --> 00:51:25.690
and then came back in the
new year and it was done,

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:27.440
which is something I
love about computers.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.880
While you have your
Christmas lunch,

00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:32.070
they can be doing
useful work for you.

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:35.870
And so we got all
the predictions.

00:51:35.870 --> 00:51:37.850
Actually, we ran this
twice to make sure,

00:51:37.850 --> 00:51:42.200
and we got all the predictions
for all of the proteins

00:51:42.200 --> 00:51:44.780
in the human proteome,
which is the equivalent

00:51:44.780 --> 00:51:46.310
of the human genome, right?

00:51:46.310 --> 00:51:48.620
So the human genome
codes for the genes,

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:49.970
which code for the proteins.

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:53.810
And there's about
20,000 proteins

00:51:53.810 --> 00:51:57.210
in the human body
expressed by our genome.

00:51:57.210 --> 00:51:58.460
So we followed them all.

00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:01.730
And just so to put
that in perspective,

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:04.160
experimentally,
after 40, 50 years

00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:07.490
of painstaking experimental
work by the entire biology

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:11.330
community, we have
determined about 17%

00:52:11.330 --> 00:52:12.740
of the human proteome.

00:52:12.740 --> 00:52:17.585
So overnight, we doubled that
to 36% with very high accuracy.

00:52:17.585 --> 00:52:21.920
And by very high accuracy, we
mean sub-angstrom accuracy,

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:25.910
so equivalent to
experimental quality.

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:30.950
And then furthermore, 58% we
determined at high accuracy.

00:52:30.950 --> 00:52:36.140
And high accuracy means that the
backbone can be trusted, right?

00:52:36.140 --> 00:52:38.240
Maybe not the side
chains, but the backbone,

00:52:38.240 --> 00:52:40.995
the general overall
structure is probably good.

00:52:40.995 --> 00:52:42.620
And that can still
be useful, maybe not

00:52:42.620 --> 00:52:44.660
for certain types of things,
not for drug discovery,

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:46.400
for example, but it
can still be useful

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:48.500
for fundamental research.

00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:50.090
And then one
interesting thing is

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:52.220
which we've had a lot
more data on since we've

00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:57.740
released all this last summer
is what about the other 42%?

00:52:57.740 --> 00:52:59.090
What's going on there?

00:52:59.090 --> 00:53:03.860
And actually, we suspected
that most of those

00:53:03.860 --> 00:53:06.680
were not errors or
deficiencies in the system,

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:08.760
but were actually indicating
something different,

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:12.560
which is that those proteins
were unstructured in isolation.

00:53:12.560 --> 00:53:15.320
So there's a lot of proteins
in the body that actually don't

00:53:15.320 --> 00:53:17.780
have a structure until they
interact with whatever they're

00:53:17.780 --> 00:53:22.190
supposed to interact with,
and they're kind of floppy

00:53:22.190 --> 00:53:24.020
before that.

00:53:24.020 --> 00:53:26.090
And actually, when
we looked into this,

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:30.380
it was very high correlation
between the known areas

00:53:30.380 --> 00:53:35.180
of intrinsic disorder and
where AlphaFold was not good

00:53:35.180 --> 00:53:37.613
and it knew it was not good.

00:53:37.613 --> 00:53:40.030
So this, taken together, is
the most complete and accurate

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:43.150
picture ever of
the human proteome.

00:53:43.150 --> 00:53:46.090
And we knew we wanted
biologists to use this,

00:53:46.090 --> 00:53:49.090
some biologists who maybe
would have no interest in how

00:53:49.090 --> 00:53:52.670
AlphaFold worked or not have the
expertise to worry about that,

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:55.570
but we wanted them to
trust the downstream data

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:57.050
and to use it in their work.

00:53:57.050 --> 00:54:00.280
So we knew that it would be
really important to create

00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:03.790
a very simple confidence
measure that biologists could

00:54:03.790 --> 00:54:06.700
look at and understand really
simply without understanding

00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:10.030
anything about the underlying
architectural details.

00:54:10.030 --> 00:54:12.818
And this is always
something we would

00:54:12.818 --> 00:54:14.860
like to have in general
with our machine learning

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:16.810
models, a kind of
measure of uncertainty

00:54:16.810 --> 00:54:20.290
and how uncertain the system
is about its own outputs.

00:54:20.290 --> 00:54:22.360
But in this case, we
thought it was critical.

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:24.820
And actually, we
developed a system that--

00:54:24.820 --> 00:54:26.680
we actually made a
slight tweak to AlphaFold

00:54:26.680 --> 00:54:29.350
that also extracted
its own confidence

00:54:29.350 --> 00:54:32.350
metric about how confident
it was about each residue

00:54:32.350 --> 00:54:33.310
position.

00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:37.600
And greater than 90 basically
means that you can rely on it,

00:54:37.600 --> 00:54:40.330
it's kind of experimental
level, and greater than 70

00:54:40.330 --> 00:54:43.300
is this high accuracy
regime, and lower than 50

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:46.060
may indicate disorder.

00:54:46.060 --> 00:54:49.290
So we founded another 20
model organisms, or the most

00:54:49.290 --> 00:54:53.680
important ones for research,
the mouse, the fruit fly,

00:54:53.680 --> 00:54:57.780
zebrafish, and then also some
major diseases, tuberculosis,

00:54:57.780 --> 00:55:02.340
and then some big plants
like rice and wheat.

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:05.970
And we're starting to cover more
and more of the biology space.

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:07.530
So we also did,
in the first half

00:55:07.530 --> 00:55:11.400
of last year created a database
with EMBL-EBI, the European

00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:13.860
Bioinformatics Institute,
based in Cambridge.

00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:17.730
They build already a lot of the
big databases, like Uniprot,

00:55:17.730 --> 00:55:20.130
and we went to them and
collaborated with them

00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:22.140
to build the AlphaFold
Protein Structure

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:24.330
Database to host all
of these structures

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:26.730
in 3D and all the
other tools and link

00:55:26.730 --> 00:55:29.310
in to all of the other
genetics tools and other tools

00:55:29.310 --> 00:55:31.140
that biologists use
every day, so it just

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:33.930
becomes part of their
everyday workflow.

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:38.880
And we did a big first release
with 330,000 total structures

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:42.120
for these 20 model organisms
plus the human proteome,

00:55:42.120 --> 00:55:44.670
and we put it out there for
free and unrestricted access

00:55:44.670 --> 00:55:46.980
for any use,
commercial, academic,

00:55:46.980 --> 00:55:50.730
freely to use it to maximize
the scientific impact

00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:53.520
and benefit to humanity.

00:55:53.520 --> 00:55:57.030
We now have more than a million
predictions in the database.

00:55:57.030 --> 00:56:00.900
We've done 440,000
annotated ones

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:02.400
from what's called Swiss-Prot.

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:07.170
We've also indexed heavily early
on neglected tropical diseases,

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:10.710
because they're often
very understudied.

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:13.128
Big pharma doesn't do
much research on them

00:56:13.128 --> 00:56:14.670
because there isn't
much money in it,

00:56:14.670 --> 00:56:16.960
but it affects millions of
people around the world,

00:56:16.960 --> 00:56:20.850
and it's terrible for their
quality of life and so on.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:23.562
So we actually work with
the WHO to prioritize--

00:56:23.562 --> 00:56:25.020
this is what we're
doing in-house--

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.780
prioritize these tropical
diseases like Chagas disease,

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:29.640
leishmaniasis, that
affect millions

00:56:29.640 --> 00:56:32.070
of people around the world
and are understudied.

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:34.080
So that means that
the nonprofits can now

00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:37.470
start with drug design
because they have the protein

00:56:37.470 --> 00:56:39.790
structures.

00:56:39.790 --> 00:56:43.750
And we've seen it already within
barely like nine months now,

00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:46.720
huge amount being done by
the community with AlphaFold

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:48.850
for modeling very
complex biology that

00:56:48.850 --> 00:56:50.230
was not modelable before.

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:53.620
So this on the left
here is an image

00:56:53.620 --> 00:56:55.870
of the nuclear pore complex.

00:56:55.870 --> 00:56:57.730
This is what allows
things in and out

00:56:57.730 --> 00:56:59.890
of your nucleus of
your cell, and it's

00:56:59.890 --> 00:57:03.170
one of the biggest
proteins in the body.

00:57:03.170 --> 00:57:04.840
It's really huge.

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:07.090
There's using AlphaFold as
a state of the art protein

00:57:07.090 --> 00:57:08.980
disorder predictor,
so many people are now

00:57:08.980 --> 00:57:12.200
using it to predict disorder.

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:14.410
Another project with
the WHO is to-- they

00:57:14.410 --> 00:57:16.450
identify the top
30 pathogens that

00:57:16.450 --> 00:57:19.270
might cause a future pandemic,
and they wanted all the protein

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:23.005
structures for those, so
we've given that to WHO.

00:57:23.005 --> 00:57:24.880
And actually, it's helped
experiment us a lot

00:57:24.880 --> 00:57:29.470
to solve their own protein
structures because sometimes

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:32.080
they have low resolution
experimental data,

00:57:32.080 --> 00:57:34.720
and if they can also use
as another source of input

00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:36.550
AlphaFold's prediction,
they can use

00:57:36.550 --> 00:57:40.480
that to combine that to
resolve a very high resolution

00:57:40.480 --> 00:57:43.485
image of actually their protein.

00:57:43.485 --> 00:57:44.860
And there's actually
now hundreds

00:57:44.860 --> 00:57:47.830
of already other papers
and applications that

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:51.730
have been using AlphaFold,
both within industry and also

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:53.320
academia.

00:57:53.320 --> 00:57:54.570
And the impact, be really big.

00:57:54.570 --> 00:57:58.020
It got listed very nicely in
the Breakthrough of the Year

00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:01.920
by Science and also Method
of the Year from Nature.

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:03.840
And the database
has been used now

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:07.110
by over 350,000 researchers.

00:58:07.110 --> 00:58:09.600
And we think there are only
about 400,000 biologists

00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:12.840
in the world, so we think
that pretty much everyone

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:16.920
has looked at the database
and is using the database.

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:21.240
And so it's super gratifying
for us to see that.

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:23.580
So now, where are we going next?

00:58:23.580 --> 00:58:27.120
There's actually over 100
million proteins or actually

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:31.090
nearly 200 million
proteins known to science.

00:58:31.090 --> 00:58:36.360
There's all these expeditions
that go to the Indian Ocean

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:40.092
or somewhere like that and they
dip a bucket into the water

00:58:40.092 --> 00:58:42.300
and you pull it out, and if
you sequence what you get

00:58:42.300 --> 00:58:44.640
you actually get whole
loads of new proteins

00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:46.530
and you stick them in
the protein databases,

00:58:46.530 --> 00:58:49.320
but no one knows the structures
of any of these things, right?

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:52.770
So now potentially, we could be
in a regime for the first time

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:57.660
ever where proteomics can
keep up with genomics, right?

00:58:57.660 --> 00:58:59.790
So you can have as many
proteins to know about,

00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:01.620
protein structures
as abundant as we

00:59:01.620 --> 00:59:03.328
know about genetic material.

00:59:03.328 --> 00:59:04.870
So that's a really
interesting thing,

00:59:04.870 --> 00:59:07.230
what then one might
do a met level

00:59:07.230 --> 00:59:09.870
about looking at this
enormous data set,

00:59:09.870 --> 00:59:15.780
and I'm very excited to see
what protein biologists end up

00:59:15.780 --> 00:59:18.210
doing with that.

00:59:18.210 --> 00:59:23.280
Yeah, so then function would
be the next thing to look at.

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:24.060
So thanks.

00:59:24.060 --> 00:59:27.000
I wanted to say thanks to
everybody, amazing teams who

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:28.390
made AlphaFold possible.

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:31.350
There's the methods team,
everybody listed on the left

00:59:31.350 --> 00:59:34.530
there, and John Jumper
leading the technical part

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:38.100
of that team and the
human proteome side

00:59:38.100 --> 00:59:40.683
and with Kathryn
leading that project.

00:59:40.683 --> 00:59:42.600
And I also want to thank,
obviously, our wider

00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:43.440
colleagues at DeepMind.

00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:45.357
Like, almost everybody
at DeepMind in some way

00:59:45.357 --> 00:59:49.080
contributed to this, and also
Alphabet, the collaborators

00:59:49.080 --> 00:59:52.740
at EMBL-EBI, CASP community,
and also the PDB, of course,

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:55.830
in the experimental
biology community who

00:59:55.830 --> 00:59:59.160
created those first 150,000
structures that then we

00:59:59.160 --> 01:00:01.500
were able to learn from.

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:04.080
And I just wanted to mention
about the augmentation part.

01:00:04.080 --> 01:00:05.940
So it turns out
one of the things

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:08.262
we had to do with AlphaFold
was do self-distillation,

01:00:08.262 --> 01:00:09.720
we're calling it
self-distillation,

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:13.080
where we actually
train on 150,000.

01:00:13.080 --> 01:00:17.380
We output some predictions
of some other proteins.

01:00:17.380 --> 01:00:21.420
And then we did a filter
on the high quality ones,

01:00:21.420 --> 01:00:24.030
or at least the ones AlphaFold
thought were higher quality,

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:26.730
and we put that back
in the training set.

01:00:26.730 --> 01:00:28.620
And actually, that helped a lot.

01:00:28.620 --> 01:00:32.850
And we needed about 250,000
to 300,000 synthetic,

01:00:32.850 --> 01:00:35.163
in this case, predictions
in the training set.

01:00:35.163 --> 01:00:37.080
It was very helpful and
quite important to get

01:00:37.080 --> 01:00:40.100
the last few percentages
out of AlphaFold.

01:00:40.100 --> 01:00:41.850
So I'm just going to
end, then, by talking

01:00:41.850 --> 01:00:44.740
a little bit, a couple
of slides on the future.

01:00:44.740 --> 01:00:48.570
So now we're working on many
other related projects, protein

01:00:48.570 --> 01:00:52.170
complexes, disorder proteins,
and point mutations,

01:00:52.170 --> 01:00:54.990
mostly dynamics of
proteins you can think

01:00:54.990 --> 01:00:56.610
of, and also protein design.

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:58.350
So there's a whole
area, I think,

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:02.130
that has been unlocked
with AlphaFold,

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:03.690
and we're going to
push further on.

01:01:03.690 --> 01:01:06.330
And others are pushing
further on all these topics.

01:01:06.330 --> 01:01:08.220
And there's just an
observation on biology,

01:01:08.220 --> 01:01:10.728
really, which is that
the more I worked on this

01:01:10.728 --> 01:01:12.270
and spent the last
five years looking

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:16.560
at the exquisite biological
system that goes on

01:01:16.560 --> 01:01:19.470
in our bodies, it's kind of
amazing to think that it can

01:01:19.470 --> 01:01:21.760
even work given its complexity.

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:23.700
And I think that one way
to understand biology

01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:26.550
is at its fundamental level,
it's an information processing

01:01:26.550 --> 01:01:27.120
system.

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:29.460
I really believe that's
the kind of fundamental way

01:01:29.460 --> 01:01:30.520
to look at it.

01:01:30.520 --> 01:01:34.920
But of course, life is
a very complex process

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:36.850
and it's an emergent process.

01:01:36.850 --> 01:01:38.640
And I think because
of that, it's

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:42.150
pretty difficult to
describe mathematically

01:01:42.150 --> 01:01:43.440
in a kind of clean way.

01:01:43.440 --> 01:01:46.380
Like, if you think about trying
to describe a whole cell,

01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:49.350
is there ever going to be
a Newton's laws of motion

01:01:49.350 --> 01:01:50.220
for a cell?

01:01:50.220 --> 01:01:51.340
I don't think so.

01:01:51.340 --> 01:01:54.390
I think it's too complicated
and it's too emergent.

01:01:54.390 --> 01:01:58.440
So in a way, it could be
the perfect regime for AI

01:01:58.440 --> 01:01:59.730
to operate in.

01:01:59.730 --> 01:02:02.748
And I think as
maths is to physics,

01:02:02.748 --> 01:02:04.290
maths is like the
perfect description

01:02:04.290 --> 01:02:06.480
language for physics,
I think AI could

01:02:06.480 --> 01:02:09.060
be the perfect description
language for biology.

01:02:09.060 --> 01:02:13.110
And I hope that AlphaFold will
be not just useful in itself,

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:15.600
but also kind of
proof of concept

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:18.810
of maybe a heralding of
a new dawn of what I call

01:02:18.810 --> 01:02:20.520
and others call digital biology.

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:24.600
And the dream would one day be
maybe to create a virtual cell

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:26.730
that one could make
useful predictions in,

01:02:26.730 --> 01:02:28.020
in silico, right?

01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:29.850
Obviously, we're
very far from that,

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:33.535
but AlphaFold is maybe
the first tentative step.

01:02:33.535 --> 01:02:35.910
And then, of course, I haven't
got time to talk about all

01:02:35.910 --> 01:02:39.030
of these things, but we've
had a really amazing year

01:02:39.030 --> 01:02:43.350
of applying these
types of AI techniques

01:02:43.350 --> 01:02:46.620
to quantum chemistry,
pure mathematics proving

01:02:46.620 --> 01:02:51.150
some pretty major conjectures,
fusion, controlling the plasma

01:02:51.150 --> 01:02:53.970
in a fusion m and genomics.

01:02:53.970 --> 01:02:56.460
And if it sounds a little bit
science fiction, this list,

01:02:56.460 --> 01:02:57.660
these are all real papers.

01:02:57.660 --> 01:02:59.730
You can go and have
a look at them.

01:02:59.730 --> 01:03:02.130
And this was always
the flourishing

01:03:02.130 --> 01:03:03.720
that I had imagined
when we started

01:03:03.720 --> 01:03:05.910
DeepMind was that
if we crack this

01:03:05.910 --> 01:03:08.790
in the right way, this kind
of diet, this kind of slide

01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:12.900
should be possible, even though
it seems like perhaps science

01:03:12.900 --> 01:03:14.310
fiction.

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:16.890
So I'll just end then
by saying in my view,

01:03:16.890 --> 01:03:21.060
AI used properly could be the
ultimate general purpose tool

01:03:21.060 --> 01:03:23.490
to help scientists see
further, much like the Hubble

01:03:23.490 --> 01:03:26.430
telescope was for
cosmologists to see further

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:27.600
into the universe.

01:03:27.600 --> 01:03:28.680
Thank you very much.

01:03:28.680 --> 01:03:30.530
[APPLAUSE]

